---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CrossLinks from '../components/CrossLinks.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="How I design with AI"
  metaTitle="How I design with AI | Tyler Young"
  description="Designing with AI means creating and managing the system it builds from: goals as planning, values as guardrails, options as working code."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="Operating Model"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Contribution', 'An operating model for directing AI to produce production code'],
    ['Role', 'Practice — not a single project'],
    ['Scope', 'Design systems, guardrails, evaluation, handoff'],
    ['Takeaway', '[NEEDS: Tyler’s one-liner]'],
  ]}
>
  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Entire page is draft copy, written from Tyler's own mapping of his workflow onto
      agentic patterns. Every sentence is his to rewrite. No claim here asserts a
      metric, a date, or a scale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="opening">
    <Draft>
      The spec that governs my agent runs to 1,048 lines. Most of it is not about
      what to build — it is about what the agent is not allowed to do, and who has
      to look before anything ships.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Specify intent, not screens</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A goal stated well is a plan the model can execute against. Stated badly, it
      produces something plausible and wrong, quickly. Most of the work that used to
      go into specifying screens now goes into specifying intent — what this surface
      is for, what it must not do, what "done" looks like.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Every value traces to the token page</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The rule I wrote: every colour, weight, radius and spacing value has to trace
      to the project's token page. If the tokens are too narrow for what is being
      asked, the agent surfaces that and waits rather than inventing a value.
      Brand guidelines and accessibility floors stop being documentation and become
      a gate the output passes before anyone looks at it.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Three options, three URLs</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      On the last engagement, three directions for one surface were built and
      deployed to three separate addresses so the choice was made against running
      software. When exploration costs almost nothing, the scarce thing is judgment
      about which option is right — so I read them, rank them, and say why. That
      part does not scale, which is the point.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Reviews happen on the rendered thing</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Options come back as working components, not pictures of components. A
      stakeholder review on a rendered surface ends an argument that a mockup review
      only postpones.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Nothing ships unread</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Code goes out with a person's name on it. The spec encodes the same asymmetry
      it asks of me: an explicit list of what the agent may do alone, and a longer
      one of what it may not — deploy, delete, restructure, or invent a value that
      is not in the system.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What this changes about the job</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A design leader's leverage used to be production capacity — how much a team
      could get drawn, reviewed and built. That constraint is loosening. What replaces
      it is the quality of the system the work comes out of: the guardrails, the
      evaluation, the standard for what is allowed to ship.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      I am not a frontend engineer, and this is not an argument that designers should
      become one. It is an argument that the systems designers own now compile.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler's closing line — the one sentence he wants a hiring manager to remember
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <CrossLinks
    slot="after"
    label="Related"
    title="The system in practice"
    links={[
      { href: '/ops-manager', label: 'The system, and the machine that builds from it' },
      { href: '/alucify', label: 'Designing for people supervising agents' },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .opening {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    margin-bottom: 34px !important;
  }
</style>
